      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Given the base-eight fraction $0.\\overline{33}_{2} = \\frac{7}{8}$, convert it into the base-two fraction $0.00110110110110\\ldots_2$. Subtract $1$ from this fraction to get $0.10101010\\ldots$, square this result to obtain $0.012121212\\dots$, multiply it by $16$ to find $0.252527 \\ldots$, and finally multiply it by $100$ to determine the $1314^{\\text{th}}$ digit.\n\nTo solve the problem, we need to find the 1314th digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\).\n\n### Step 1: Find the Decimal Expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\)\n\nFirst, we perform the long division of 5 by 14 to determine the decimal expansion.\n\n\\[\n5 \\div 14 = 0.0357142857142857\\ldots\n\\]\n\nFrom this, we see that the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\) is \\(0.0357142857142857\\ldots\\).\n\n### Step 2: Identify the Period of the Decimal Expansion\n\nWe notice that the decimal repeats every 6 digits:\n\\[\n0.\\overline{357142}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Determine the Position of the 1314th Digit\n\nTo find the position of the 1314th digit, we need to divide 1314 by the length of the repeating cycle (6).\n\n\\[\n1314 \\div 6 = 219 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 0\n\\]\n\nThis tells us that 1314 is exactly at the end of a complete cycle of the repeating digit sequence \\(357142\\). Therefore, the 1314th digit corresponds to the last digit of this cycle.\n\n### Step 4: Identify the Last Digit of the Cycle\n\nThe repeating cycle is \\(357142\\), and the last digit (6th digit in the cycle) is 2.\n\n### Step 5: Conclude the Final Digit\n\nThus, the 1314th digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
