      "text": "To find the 1314th digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\), we first need to determine the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\).\n\nPerforming the division, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{14} = 0.\\overline{357142}\n\\]\nThis means the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\) is a repeating decimal with the repeating block \"357142\". The length of the repeating block is 6 digits.\n\nTo find the 1314th digit in the repeating sequence, we can use modular arithmetic. Specifically, we need to find the position of the digit within the repeating block. We do this by computing \\(1314 \\mod 6\\):\n\n\\[\n1314 \\div 6 = 219 \\text{ remainder } 0\n\\]\nSo, \\(1314 \\mod 6 = 0\\). This tells us that the 1314th digit corresponds to the last digit of the repeating block \"357142\", which is \"2\".\n\nTherefore, the 1314th digit past the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{5}{14}\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
