      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Break $\\dfrac{5}{14} = \\dfrac{3}{2}\\cdot\\dfrac{5}{7}$, tune the two inconspicuous fractions to get\n$$\\dfrac{3}{2} = \\dfrac{3}{3\\cdot1} = \\dfrac{1}{1} + \\dfrac{2}{3} - \\dfrac{5}{6} + \\dfrac{4}{9} - \\dfrac{3}{12} + \\dfrac{2}{15} - \\dfrac{1}{18} + \\dfrac{0}{21} + \\dfrac{1}{24} - \\cdots = 1 + \\dfrac{2}{3} - \\dfrac{5}{6} + \\dfrac{4}{9} = 2 - \\dfrac{1}{6} = \\dfrac{11}{6},$$ and\n$\\dfrac{5}{7} = \\dfrac{3}{6} + \\dfrac{2}{7} - \\dfrac{5}{10} + \\dfrac{4}{14} = 1 + \\dfrac{2}{5} - \\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{2}{7} = \\dfrac{7}{5} - \\dfrac{12}{10} = \\dfrac{7}{5} - \\dfrac{6}{5} = \\dfrac{1}{5}$,\nthus $\\dfrac{5}{14} = \\dfrac{11}{6} - \\dfrac{1}{5}$. Our goal is to find the $1314^{\\text{th}}$ digit past the decimal point in $\\dfrac{11}{6}$. For simplicity, we express it as\n$$\\dfrac{11}{6} = 1 + \\dfrac{5}{6} = 1 - \\dfrac{1}{6}.$$\nThe orbit of $\\dfrac{5}{6}$ is $\\dfrac{5}{6}\\rightarrow\\dfrac{1}{6}\\rightarrow\\dfrac{7}{6}\\rightarrow\\dfrac{1}{6}\\rightarrow\\dfrac{5}{6}\\rightarrow\\dfrac{1}{6}\\rightarrow\\dots$, so the sequence repeats after each period of four. Every $4^{\\text{th}}$ digit past the decimal point is $7$.\nHence, to find the $1314^{\\text{th}}$ digit past the decimal point in $\\dfrac{5}{14}$, we find the remainder of dividing $1314$ by $4$:\n\n$$\\dfrac{1314}{4} = 328 \\text{ }\\text{with}\\text{ }\\text{remainder}\\text{ }\\boxed{2}.$$\nSo, the $1314^{\\text{th}}$ digit past the decimal point in $\\dfrac{5}{14}$ is $\\boxed{7}$.",
